##########################################################################
# (C4) CRITICAL HONESTY CAVEAT: Lemma L is TIE-BREAK / CONVENTION DEPENDENT
##########################################################################
The "apex regular <=> full transversal" lemma is NOT a pure residue-combinatorial
fact. Regularity of R_13 requires the ANTIPODAL (delta=7) and doubled-residue
(delta=0) TIES to be oriented ROTATIONALLY (the natural-speed tie-break that a
real tight set induces). Findings (exact):
  * Full transversal {1..13} is regular under only 1 of 64 antipodal tie-break
    orientations (the rotational one). The natural integer speeds 1..13 give
    exactly that one => AP is regular all-6.
  * Under the NATURAL-speed convention (present residues at face value, the moved
    integer at +14), 0 of all 1-missing and 2-missing residue multisets are
    regular. So "apex regular => full transversal" HOLDS for the actual induced
    apex tournaments.
  * But abstractly (allowing ANY tie orientation), 420 of 5148 two-missing
    residue multisets CAN be made regular by SOME tie orientation. So the
    iso-class statement "regular tournament => transversal residues" is FALSE in
    general; it is only the natural-convention induced map that is injective on
    the transversal.
CONSEQUENCE: tournament rigidity proves the residue transversal for the AP branch
ONLY under the (correct, tight-set-induced) natural-speed apex convention; it is
not a convention-free tournament-iso-class theorem. The magnitude/tie-break data
that fixes the convention is exactly the integer information the apex is otherwise
blind to -- so the gap to the integer-level lemma is intrinsic.
